/-!
# Permanence under henselization: flatness, smoothness, and reducedness

Stacks Project, Chapter 15 "More on Algebra". The tags treated here are

07QM, 07QN, 06DH, 0ASE.

## Tag 07QM — Elementary properties of henselizations

The henselization and strict henselization of a local ring are faithfully flat, their maximal
ideals are generated by the maximal ideal of the original ring, and henselization does not change
quotients by powers of that ideal. The corresponding quotients of the strict henselization are
free modules, with compatible bases obtained by reducing one family of elements.

### Main definitions

No new definitions are introduced.

### Main results

* `faithfullyFlat_and_map_maximalIdeal_of_isHenselization`: flatness and the
  maximal-ideal equality for each henselization separately.
* `henselization_faithfullyFlat_and_quotient_basis`: the faithfully flatness,
  maximal-ideal, quotient, and freeness properties of henselizations.

### Implementation notes

Since henselizations are not primitive types in Mathlib, `Rh` and `Rsh` are characterized by the
ind-étale, henselian, local, and residue-field properties used by their universal properties. The
quotient isomorphism is expressed as bijectivity of its canonical ring homomorphism. Freeness on
the specified reductions is expressed by a basis whose value at each index is that reduction.

Flatness and the maximal-ideal equalities follow from the ind-étale presentations. The quotient
isomorphisms and compatible bases follow by lifting modulo nilpotent ideals in the flat
quotients; the required lifting helpers are private to this module.

### References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 07QM](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07QM)

## Tag 07QN — Formal smoothness of henselizations

The henselization and strict henselization of a local ring are formally étale over the
appropriate preceding rings. They are consequently formally smooth when their targets carry
their maximal-ideal-adic topologies and their sources carry the discrete topologies.

### Main definitions

Henselizations and strict henselizations are expressed by
`IsLocalRing.IsHenselization`.

### Main results

* `henselization_formallyEtale_and_isFormallySmooth`: the three maps in the tower from
  a local ring to its henselization and strict henselization are formally étale and formally
  smooth for the indicated adic topologies.

### Implementation notes

The source's rings `R^h` and `R^{sh}` are represented by arbitrary rings satisfying the
corresponding henselization predicates. The local `R^h`-algebra structure on `R^{sh}` identifies
the canonical middle map. Each topological formal-smoothness assertion installs the discrete
topology on its source and the adic topology defined by the maximal ideal of its target.

### References

* [StacksProject 07QN](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07QN)

## Tag 06DH — Reducedness of henselizations

A local ring is reduced if and only if its henselization is reduced, and if and only if any
strict henselization associated to a separable closure of its residue field is reduced.

### Main definitions

Henselizations and strict henselizations are expressed by
`IsLocalRing.IsHenselization`.

### Main results

* `isReduced_tfae_henselization_strictHenselization` gives the three equivalent
  reducedness conditions.

### Implementation notes

The strict henselization is represented as a henselization whose prescribed residue field is a
separable closure of the residue field of the base local ring. This avoids choosing particular
models for either henselization.

### References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 06DH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06DH)

## Tag 0ASE — Nilradicals of henselizations

For a local ring, extension of its nilradical to a henselization or a strict henselization is
the nilradical of that ring.

### Main definitions

There are no new definitions.

### Main results

* `nilradical_map_eq_henselization_and_strictHenselization` identifies the
  nilradicals of a henselization and a strict henselization with the extensions of the
  nilradical of the base ring.

### Implementation notes

Henselizations and strict henselizations are represented by arbitrary models satisfying
`IsLocalRing.IsHenselization`; the residue field prescribed for the strict henselization is a
separable closure of the residue field of the base ring.

### References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0ASE](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ASE)

-/


